\begin{abstract}
Using elementary techniques from Geometric Analysis, Partial Differential Equations, and Abelian $C^*$ Algebras, we uncover a novel, yet familiar, global geometric discriminant -- namely the indexed set of integrals of triple products of eigenfunctions of the Schr\"odinger/Witten Operator, to precisely characterize which isospectral Schr\"odinger/Witten Operators have isometric Riemannian geometries with matching potentials/weights.

\noindent\textbf{Keywords:} spectrum, Fourier, harmonic, triple products, Schr\"odinger, eigenfunctions, functional, inverse problem, representation theory
\end{abstract}

\tableofcontents

\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:intro}

Let $(M,g)$ be a smooth, connected, complete Riemannian manifold (possibly non-compact, possibly of infinite volume). Fix a real potential $V\in C^\infty(M)$ which is bounded below and proper in the sense
$$V(x)\to +\infty\qquad\text{as }x\to\infty$$
(i.e., $\{x:V(x)\le C\}$ is compact for every $C$). Let
$$H=-\Delta_g+V$$
be the associated Schr\"odinger operator, essentially self-adjoint on $C_c^\infty(M)$. Under these hypotheses it is classical that

$\sigma(H)=\sigma_{\mathrm{disc}}(H)=\{\lambda_0<\lambda_1\le\lambda_2\le\cdots\}\to+\infty$, each eigenvalue of finite multiplicity;
there is an orthonormal basis $\{e^i\}_{i=0}^\infty\subset L^2(M,d\mathrm{vol}_g)$ of smooth eigenfunctions,$$He^i=\lambda_i e^i.$$

(The same conclusions hold for $-\Delta_g$ itself on some complete manifolds of finite volume with no continuous spectrum, but the confining potential makes the analysis cleaner.)
Define the triple products exactly as in the compact case:
$$M^{i,j,k}:=\langle e^i e^j,e^k\rangle_{L^2}=\int_M e^i e^j\,\overline{e^k}\,d\mathrm{vol}_g.$$
Because the eigenfunctions are Schwartz-class at infinity in the sense that they decay faster than any polynomial in $V$ (Agmon estimates \cite{Agmon1982}/ weighted Sobolev \cite{TaylorPDE1}), the products $e^i e^j$ lie in $L^2$ and the integrals converge absolutely.

\subsection{Results}
\label{subsec:res}

\begin{theorem}
\label{thm}

Let $(M,g,V)$ and $(N,h,W)$ be two such data, with isospectral Schrödinger operators $H_M$ and $H_N$. Suppose there exist orthonormal eigenbases $\{e^i\}$ of $H_M$ and $\{f^i\}$ of $H_N$, ordered by non-decreasing eigenvalues, such that
\begin{equation}
M_M^{i,j,k}=M_N^{i,j,k}\qquad\forall\qquad i,j,k.
\end{equation}
Then there is a diffeomorphism $F:N\to M$ with $F^*g=h$ and $V\circ F=W$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{corollary}
\label{coro}
Let $(M,g)$ be a complete connected Riemannian manifold and $\varphi\in C^\infty(M)$ a weight such that the Witten operator
$$L_\varphi u=-\Delta_g u+\langle\nabla\varphi,\nabla u\rangle$$
is essentially self-adjoint and bounded below on $C_c^\infty(M)$, with
$$\sigma(L_\varphi)=\sigma_{\mathrm{disc}}(L_\varphi)=\{\nu_0=0<\nu_1\le\nu_2\le\cdots\}\to+\infty$$
in $L^2(M,e^{-\varphi}\,d\mathrm{vol}_g)$. (This holds whenever $\varphi$ is confining in the Bakry–Émery sense: e.g. $\operatorname{Ric}_g+\operatorname{Hess}\varphi\ge K$ and $\varphi(x)\to+\infty$ as $x\to\infty$.) Let $\{f^i\}_{i=0}^\infty$ be an orthonormal eigenbasis,
$$L_\varphi f^i=\nu_i f^i,\qquad
\int_M f^i\overline{f^j}\,e^{-\varphi}\,d\mathrm{vol}_g=\delta^{ij},$$
ordered by nondecreasing eigenvalues, and set
$$W^{i,j,k}:=\int_M f^i f^j\overline{f^k}\,e^{-\varphi}\,d\mathrm{vol}_g=\langle f^if^j,f^k\rangle_{L^2(e^{-\varphi}\mathrm{vol})}.$$
Let $(N,h,\psi)$ be a second such triple, with Witten operator $L_\psi$ isospectral to $L_\varphi$, and suppose there is an eigenbasis $\{u^i\}$ of $L_\psi$ with the same eigenvalues and
$$\int_N u^i u^j\overline{u^k}\,e^{-\psi}\,d\mathrm{vol}_h=W^{i,j,k}
\quad\text{for all }i,j,k.$$
Then there exists a diffeomorphism $F:N\to M$ such that
$$F^*g=h,\qquad \psi=\varphi\circ F+\log \frac{\mu(M)}{\mu(N)}.$$
In particular the weighted Riemannian manifolds $(M,g,e^{-\varphi}\mathrm{vol}_g)$ and $(N,h,e^{-\psi}\mathrm{vol}_h)$ are isometric.
\end{corollary}

\begin{remark}
The Witten Laplacian on $p$-forms is the Hodge Laplacian of the weighted manifold plus $t\operatorname{Hess}\varphi\#_p+t^2\lvert\nabla\varphi\rvert^2$. Once $(M,g,\varphi)$ is reconstructed from the scalar array $W^{i,j,k}$, the operators $L_{\varphi}^{(p)}$ are determined, so their spectra and eigenform pairings against scalar eigenfunctions are invariants, not additional moduli.
\end{remark}
\section{Preliminaries}
\label{sec:pre}

Let $\mathcal{A}_0\subset L^2(M)$ be the algebraic span of the eigenfunctions. Finite linear combinations
$$\phi=\sum_{i=0}^N\hat\phi(i)\,e^i$$ are dense in $L^2$ and, by elliptic regularity plus the decay of eigenfunctions, lie in $C^\infty\cap C_0(M)$. Pointwise multiplication is
$$\phi\psi=\sum_k\Bigl(\sum_{i,j}\hat\phi(i)\hat\psi(j)M^{i,j,k}\Bigr)e^k.$$
Thus the structure constants determine the product of any two finite eigenfunction expansions. The same identity holds on $N$. Consequently the linear map
$$\vec F:\mathcal{A}_0(M)\to\mathcal{A}_0(N),\qquad e^i\mapsto f^i$$
is an algebra homomorphism for pointwise multiplication (the constant function is a multiple of the ground state only if $V$ is constant; in general $\vec F$ need not send $1$ to $1$, but it preserves the product of any two functions that can be so expanded). Characteristic functions of sublevel sets of $V$ can be approximated in $L^2$ by functional calculus of $H$, so $\vec F$ also preserves characteristic functions of sets of finite measure in the $L^2$ sense.
This is identical to the compact calculation; the only extra ingredient is that the eigenfunction expansion of a compactly supported smooth function still converges rapidly enough for the triple-product formula to be justified (which follows from the same elliptic estimates that give $\lambda_i^{N}\|e^i\|_\infty\to 0$ locally, plus Agmon decay at infinity).

\section{Proof of Theorem}
\begin{proof}
(Following \cite{JS24}) For sufficiency, we now consider the linear, bijective orthonormal eigenfunction basis map $\vec{F}$ from $C^\infty(M)$ to $C^\infty(N)$ and note that from the calculations in Section~\ref{sec:pre} above, $\vec{F}$ preserves pointwise products for smooth functions (and preserves characteristic functions when extended to $L^2(M,g)$) by the premise that $\set{M^{i,j,k}}$ is invariant under this map.

\begin{lemma}
\label{lma}
 $\vec F$ preserves the uniform norm on the span of the eigenfunctions, hence extends to an isometric $*$-homomorphism
$$\vec F:C_0(M)\supset\overline{\mathcal{A}_0}^{\|\cdot\|_\infty}\longrightarrow C_0(N).$$
\end{lemma}

\begin{subproof}[Proof of Lemma~\ref{lma}]
On a compact manifold one uses a smooth partition of unity and the fact that $\|a\|_\infty=1$ for a partition function $a$ is detected by $\|a^p\|_\infty=1$ for all $p$, which is visible in the Fourier coefficients because the limiting characteristic function of $\{a=1\}$ has $L^2$-mass.
In the non-compact setting replace a global partition of unity by a sequence of cutoffs $\chi_R$ that equal $1$ on $\{V\le R\}$ and vanish outside $\{V\le R+1\}$. On each such “almost-compact” region the same argument applies: if $\|\phi\|_\infty=c$ is attained on a set of positive measure inside $\{V\le R\}$, the powers $\phi^p/\|\phi\|_\infty^p$ converge in $L^2_{\mathrm{loc}}$ to a characteristic function, and $\vec F$ preserves that limit because it preserves all triple products. Sending $R\to\infty$ and using that every function in $C_0(M)$ is uniformly small outside a large sublevel set of $V$ gives $\|\vec F(\phi)\|_\infty=\|\phi\|_\infty$.

\end{subproof}

Now we apply the Gelfand-Naimark Representation Theorem (in contravariant functor form) for Abelian $C^*$ algebras $C_0(N), C_0(M)$ \cite{JC19} to represent this isomorphism $\vec{F}$ by a homeomorphism $F$ between $N$ and $M$. Since $\vec{F}$ is bijective on smooth functions, $F$ too must be smooth.

As this now diffeomorphism preserves eigenvalues and eigenfunctions (by hypothesis on $\vec{F}(f) = f \circ F$), it must preserve the Schr\"odinger operator on smooth functions. By construction
$$\vec F(\phi)=\phi\circ F$$
on the eigenbasis, hence on all finite combinations. Therefore
$$H_N(\phi\circ F)=(H_M\phi)\circ F$$
for every eigenfunction $\phi$, and by density for every Schwartz-class function. In particular $F$ pulls eigenfunctions of $H_M$ back to eigenfunctions of $H_N$ with the same eigenvalues.
Writing $H=-\Delta+V$ in local coordinates, the principal symbol of $H$ is the same as that of $-\Delta$, namely the cometric $g^{ij}\xi_i\xi_j$. An elliptic operator that is intertwined by a diffeomorphism has its principal symbol pulled back. Hence
$$F^*g=h.$$
The zeroth-order terms then give $V\circ F=W$.

This completes the proof of the Theorem.
\end{proof}

\begin{subproof}[Sketch of Proof of Corollary~\ref{coro}]
Regularity of the Witten eigenbases and corresponding $W^{i,j,k}$ follows from the unitary equivalence to the Schr\"odinger case via $H=e^{-\varphi/2}L_\varphi e^{\varphi/2}$, whose potential is $V=\frac14\lvert\nabla\varphi\rvert^2-\frac12\Delta\varphi$ (up to the additive $\lambda_0$). The rest is a rehash of the Gelfand-Naimark construction on the Witten eigenbases.
\end{subproof}



\section{Example}
On $M=\mathbb{R}$ with the Euclidean metric, take
$$H=-\frac{d^2}{dx^2}+x^2.$$
The potential $V(x)=x^2$ is smooth, bounded below, and $V(x)\to+\infty$ as $|x|\to\infty$. Hence $\sigma_{\mathrm{ess}}(H)=\emptyset$. The spectrum is
$$\lambda_n=2n+1,\qquad n=0,1,2,\dots$$
(each of multiplicity one). The corresponding $L^2(\mathbb{R})$-normalized eigenfunctions are the Hermite functions
$$\psi_n(x)=\bigl(2^n n!\,\sqrt{\pi}\bigr)^{-1/2}H_n(x)\,e^{-x^2/2},$$
where $H_n$ are the physicist’s Hermite polynomials. They form an orthonormal basis of $L^2(\mathbb{R})$, lie in the Schwartz class $\mathcal{S}(\mathbb{R})\subset C_0(\mathbb{R})$, and satisfy the Agmon decay that makes every pointwise product $\psi_i\psi_j$ square-integrable.
\subsection{Triple products}
Because the eigenfunctions are real,
$$M^{i,j,k}=\langle\psi_i\psi_j,\psi_k\rangle=\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}\psi_i(x)\psi_j(x)\psi_k(x)\,dx.$$
These integrals vanish unless $i+j+k$ is even (parity) and the three indices satisfy a triangle-type constraint coming from the linearization of Hermite polynomials. The linearization formula
$$H_n(x)H_m(x)=\sum_{r=0}^{\min(n,m)}\binom{n}{r}\binom{m}{r}r!\,2^r\,H_{n+m-2r}(x)$$
together with the Gaussian weight converts, after normalizing, into an explicit formula for $M^{i,j,k}$. Equivalently, the generating-function identity
$$\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}e^{-x^2}H_a(x)H_b(x)H_c(x)\,dx
=\sqrt{\pi}\,2^N\frac{a!\,b!\,c!}{(N-a)!(N-b)!(N-c)!}$$
(when $a+b+c=2N$ is even and each factorial in the denominator is defined and non-negative, and $0$ otherwise) yields $M^{i,j,k}$ after inserting the normalization constants of the $\psi_n$. In particular:

$M^{n,n,0}=\int\psi_n^2\psi_0\,dx>0$ (the ground state $\psi_0=\pi^{-1/4}e^{-x^2/2}$ is a strictly positive Gaussian);
the map $\psi_i\psi_j=\sum_k M^{i,j,k}\psi_k$ is the exact multiplication rule in the Hermite basis.

So the structure constants of pointwise multiplication on the dense subspace $\mathrm{span}\{\psi_n\}\subset C_0(\mathbb{R})$ are known in closed form.
\subsection{What the theorem says}
Suppose $(\mathbb{R},g,W)$ is another complete Riemannian line (so $g=e^{2\phi(x)}\,dx^2$ in some coordinate) with a smooth confining potential $W$, and suppose its Schrödinger operator $-\Delta_g+W$ is isospectral to $H$ and admits an eigenbasis $\{f_n\}$ with the same triple products
$$\langle f_i f_j,f_k\rangle=M^{i,j,k}.$$
The discrete-spectrum argument then produces a diffeomorphism $F:\mathbb{R}\to\mathbb{R}$ such that
$$F^*g=dx^2,\qquad W\circ F=x^2.$$
In one dimension this is almost tautological once the algebra is identified, but the mechanism is visible by hand:

Finite Hermite combinations are dense in $C_0(\mathbb{R})$ for the uniform norm (Stone–Weierstrass on each compact plus Gaussian decay at infinity).
The numbers $M^{i,j,k}$ determine the product of any two such combinations, hence determine a $C^*$-isomorphism$$C_0(\mathbb{R})\longrightarrow C_0(\mathbb{R}),\qquad \psi_n\mapsto f_n.$$
Gelfand–Naimark supplies a homeomorphism of the underlying locally compact spaces; smoothness of the eigenfunctions upgrades it to a diffeomorphism $F$.
Intertwining of $H$ forces the principal symbol (the cometric) and the zeroth-order term (the potential) to match.

Because every eigenspace is one-dimensional, the singular-value packaging collapses to the absolute values $|M^{i,j,k}|$ together with a consistent choice of signs (the “diagonal litmus” of the compact paper: the products $M^{i,i,k}$ determine the even part of the algebra and fix most signs).

\subsection{Explicit Formula}
$$M^{i,j,k}=\langle\psi_i\psi_j,\psi_k\rangle
=\int_{\mathbb{R}}\psi_i(x)\psi_j(x)\psi_k(x)\,dx
=N_i N_j N_k\int_{\mathbb{R}}H_i(x)H_j(x)H_k(x)\,e^{-3x^2/2}\,dx,$$
where $N_n=(2^n n!\sqrt{\pi})^{-1/2}$.

$M^{i,j,k}=0$ unless both of the following hold -- parity: $i+j+k$ is even; triangle: $|i-j|\le k\le i+j$ (and cyclic permutations).

These are the oscillator analogues of the Clebsch–Gordan support conditions.

Change variables $x=\sqrt{2/3}\,y$. Then
$$\int_{\mathbb{R}}H_i(x)H_j(x)H_k(x)\,e^{-3x^2/2}\,dx
=\sqrt{\frac{2}{3}}\int_{\mathbb{R}}H_i\Bigl(\sqrt{\tfrac{2}{3}}\,y\Bigr)
H_j\Bigl(\sqrt{\tfrac{2}{3}}\,y\Bigr)
H_k\Bigl(\sqrt{\tfrac{2}{3}}\,y\Bigr)\,e^{-y^2}\,dy.$$
Each scaled polynomial expands by the finite identity
$$H_n(\gamma y)=\sum_{\ell=0}^{\lfloor n/2\rfloor}\gamma^{n-2\ell}(\gamma^2-1)^\ell\binom{n}{2\ell}\frac{(2\ell)!}{\ell!}\,H_{n-2\ell}(y),$$
with $\gamma=\sqrt{2/3}$. The product of the three expansions is a finite linear combination of products $H_a H_b H_c$. Titchmarsh’s formula \cite{T48} then evaluates every remaining integral:
$$\int_{\mathbb{R}}H_a H_b H_c\,e^{-y^2}\,dy
=
\begin{cases}
\dfrac{\sqrt{\pi}\,2^{s}\,a!\,b!\,c!}{(s-a)!(s-b)!(s-c)!}
& \text{if }a+b+c=2s\text{ is even and }s\ge a,b,c,\\
0 & \text{otherwise.}
\end{cases}$$
The result is a finite triple sum (at most $O(ijk)$ terms, typically far fewer). Combining the prefactors $N_i N_j N_k\sqrt{2/3}$ gives $M^{i,j,k}$ exactly.

Equivalently, the product of two normalized functions expands in a scaled Hermite basis
$$\psi_i(x)\psi_j(x)=\sum_{r=0}^{\lfloor(i+j)/2\rfloor}c_{r;ij}\,\psi_{i+j-2r}(\sqrt{2}\,x),$$
with explicit coefficients
$$c_{r;ij}
=\frac{(-1)^r}{\sqrt{2^{i+j-2r+1}\pi}}
\sqrt{\frac{(i+j-2r)!}{i!\,j!}}
\binom{i}{r}\binom{j}{r}r!\,2^{r}$$
(up to the conventional placement of the $\sqrt{2}$ in the argument; cf. \cite{K16}). Then
$$M^{i,j,k}=\sum_{r}c_{r;ij}\int_{\mathbb{R}}\psi_{i+j-2r}(\sqrt{2}\,x)\,\psi_k(x)\,dx,$$
and each scaled overlap is a ${}_2F_1$ (or terminates as a short hypergeometric sum). This is the form best suited to computation.

\section{Gamma Calculus}
By definition \cite{BE85}
$$\Gamma_H(f,g):=\frac12\bigl(H(fg)-f\,Hg-g\,Hf\bigr).$$
On basis vectors this is already diagonal in the spectrum:
$$\Gamma_H(e^i,e^j)=\frac12\sum_k(\lambda_k-\lambda_i-\lambda_j)\,M^{i,j,k}\,e^k.$$
Hence
$$\begin{aligned}
\langle\nabla e^i,\nabla e^j\rangle
&=-\Gamma_H(e^i,e^j)-\frac12 V e^i e^j\\
&=-\frac12\sum_k[(\lambda_k-\lambda_i-\lambda_j)M^{i,j,k} +\int V e^i e^j \bar e^k\, dx]\, e^k.
\end{aligned}$$

Ground-state conjugation repairs the $V$ defect: if $H\psi=\lambda_0\psi$ with $\psi>0$, then

$$L:=\psi^{-1}(H-\lambda_0)(\psi\,\cdot\,)=-\Delta-2\langle\nabla\log\psi,\nabla\,\cdot\,\rangle$$
is a diffusion, and its Bakry–Émery tensor is $\mathrm{Ric}_L=\operatorname{Ric}-2\operatorname{Hess}\log\psi$. For the harmonic oscillator the ground state is a Gaussian, $\log\psi_0=-x^2/2+\mathrm{const}$, and one recovers exactly $\mathrm{BE}(2,\infty)$ for this renormalized Ornstein–Uhlenbeck process — the classical diffusion underneath the quantum oscillator. In particular, $\mathrm{Ric}_L\ge K g$ implies the spectral gap bound $\lambda_1-\lambda_0\ge K$, with equality for the harmonic oscillator.

With $\phi =- 2 \log \psi_0$ and $v\cdot w$ the Riemannian cometric, let us write these operators in the eigenbasis of $L$,

\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
f_n&=\frac{\psi_n}{\psi_0},\qquad
 W^{i,j,k}=\int\frac{\psi_i\psi_j\psi_k}{\psi_0}\,d\mathrm{vol},\\
\Gamma_L(f_i,f_j):&=\frac12\sum_k(\lambda_k+\lambda_0-\lambda_i-\lambda_j)W^{i,j,k}\,f_k = -df_i \cdot df_j\\
\Gamma_{2,L}(f_i,f_j):&=\frac14\sum_k(\lambda_k+\lambda_0-\lambda_i-\lambda_j)^2 W^{i,j,k}\,f_k\implies\\
\Gamma_{2,L}(f)&=\lvert\operatorname{Hess}f\rvert_{\mathrm{HS}}^2+\operatorname{Ric}(\nabla f,\nabla f) + \operatorname{Hess}\phi(\nabla f, \nabla f).
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}

For a Schr\"odinger operator as in the Theorem~\ref{thm}, the isometric type of $(M,g,V)$ is encoded in the diffusion data $(\{\lambda_n-\lambda_0\},\{W^{i,j,k}\})$, because $\Gamma_L$ is the cometric and $\psi_0$ recovers $V$ by Riccati.

\subsection{Returning to Harmonic Oscillator Example}
With $f_n=\psi_n/\psi_0$ and $d\mu=\psi_0^2\,dx$,
$$W^{i,j,k}=\int_{\mathbb{R}}\frac{\psi_i\psi_j\psi_k}{\psi_0}\,dx
=\frac{N_i N_j N_k}{N_0}\int H_i H_j H_k\,e^{-x^2}\,dx.$$
Titchmarsh \cite{T48} then collapses to
$$W^{i,j,k}
=
\frac{\sqrt{i!\,j!\,k!}}{(s-i)!(s-j)!(s-k)!}
\quad\text{when }i+j+k=2s\text{ and }s\ge i,j,k,$$
and $0$ otherwise. In particular $W^{0,n,n}=1$, which is orthonormality of $\{\psi_n\}$ in $L^2(dx)$.

As these structure constants are non-negative on a multiplicity-1 spectrum, they are the associated singular-value invariants \cite{JS24} for $L$.

\subsection{Schr\"odinger and Hyperbolic Surfaces}
Consider the Schr\"odinger Operator family over $Y(1)=(\Gamma = SL(2,\mathbb{Z}))\backslash\mathbb{H}$ parametrized by $\alpha > 0$:
$$H_\alpha=-\Delta_g+\alpha y^2,\qquad
M^{i,j,k}(\alpha)=\int_{Y(1)}e^i_\alpha e^j_\alpha\overline{e^k_\alpha}\,d\mathrm{vol}.$$

For each $\alpha$, $\{e^n_\alpha\}$ is an orthonormal eigenbasis of a confining Schrödinger operator on a complete orbifold of finite volume. Theorem 1 applies: the pair $$\bigl(\{\lambda_n(\alpha)\},\{M^{i,j,k}(\alpha)\}\bigr)$$ determines $(g_{\mathrm{hyp}},\alpha y^2)$ up to isometry. In particular it determines $\alpha$, because $V=\alpha y^2$ is recovered as the zeroth-order term after the metric is known.
The arrays for different $\alpha$ are not related by a unitary that preserves products. Multiplication by $y$ does not commute with $H_\alpha$, so
$$M^{i,j,k}(\alpha)\;\neq\;M^{i,j,k}(\alpha').$$
The path $\alpha\mapsto M(\alpha)$ is a curve in the space of structure constants of $C_0(Y(1))$.
On the strip $y\ge 1$,
$$e^n_\alpha(x+iy)=\sum_{m\in\mathbb{Z}}f_m^{(n)}(y)\,e^{2\pi imx}.$$
Each $f_m^{(n)}$ solves the 1D problem
$$T_{m,\alpha}f=\lambda_n(\alpha)f,\qquad
T_{m,\alpha}=-y^2\partial_{yy}+\bigl((2\pi m)^2+\alpha\bigr)y^2$$
up to the matching conditions on the arc $|z|=1$. The product of two eigenfunctions has coefficients that are convolutions
$$(e^i e^j)_m(y)=\sum_{p+q=m}f_p^{(i)}(y)\,f_q^{(j)}(y).$$
The triple product splits as core plus cusp:
$$M^{i,j,k}(\alpha)
=\int_{\{y\le 1\}}e^i e^j\overline{e^k}\,\frac{dx\,dy}{y^2}
+\sum_m\int_1^\infty f_m^{(i)}f_m^{(j)}\overline{f_m^{(k)}}\,\frac{dy}{y^2}.$$
The integral over the compact core $\{y\le 1\}$ is the part that needs a 2D solver (or a finite-element scheme on the triangle).
Because of Agmon decay, $f_m^{(n)}(y)$ is $O(e^{-c\sqrt{\alpha}\,y})$ uniformly in bounded $m$. For large $\alpha$ the cusp contribution to $M^{i,j,k}$ is negligible: the mass of every low eigenfunction sits near $y=\sqrt{3}/2$. For small $\alpha$ the cusp tail of the ground state is long, and the $m=0$ integral
$$\int_1^\infty f_0^{(0)}(y)^3\,\frac{dy}{y^2}$$
is a definite fraction of $M^{0,0,0}(\alpha)$.
Parity and the circle action $x\mapsto x+1$ force
$$M^{i,j,k}(\alpha)=0$$
unless the total winding of the three leading cusp modes can cancel. If one truncates each $e^n_\alpha$ to frequencies $|m|\le M$, the product only feeds modes $|m|\le 2M$. That is the modular analogue of the Hermite triangle rule, except the “triangle” is additive in the cusp frequency $m$, not in the energy index $n$.
There is no exact vanishing $M^{i,j,k}=0$ from $\lambda_i+\lambda_j-\lambda_k$; the potential $\alpha y^2$ breaks the pure hyperbolic selection rules of Maass-form triple products.

The spectrum of $H_\alpha$ is simple for generic $\alpha$ (no extra symmetry beyond the involution $x\mapsto-x$). Products $M^{i,i,k}(\alpha)$ fix signs. The involution does produce even/odd sectors; so we must restrict the diagonal litmus test in \cite{JS24} to each sector or use real even/odd bases.
As $\alpha\to\infty$, the eigenfunctions look like oscillator states on the tangent plane. Then $M^{i,j,k}(\alpha)$ approach the Hermite triple products of the 2D oscillator (Titchmarsh), times $\alpha^{1/4}$ Jacobian factors from the local chart.

$\alpha\mapsto(\lambda(\alpha),M(\alpha))$ is a path of reconstructed data for a fixed underlying surface with a varying potential. Reconstruction does not see a new metric as $\alpha$ changes; it sees the same $g$ and a rescaled $V$. If two modular surfaces (different $\Gamma$) were compared at the same $\alpha$, mismatch of $M^{1,1,k}(\alpha)$ for one small $k$ would differentiate them isometrically.

